e": "The polling average puts Jackson up about two points, and markets plus several quantitative raters lean Democratic, but Collins's incumbency, fundraising edge, and a string of polls showing her tied or slightly ahead keep this genuinely close. A modest Democratic edge of about 2.5 points best balances those signals.", "watch": "A few more high-quality polls in October, especially on turnout and the abortion and immigration flashpoints, could settle whether Jackson's edge is real or Collins consolidates her incumbency advantage.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Susan Collins (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Maine's Senate race is one of the closest contests of the cycle, with Senator Susan Collins seeking another term against Maine Senate President Troy Jackson. Recent polls split between a narrow Jackson lead and a narrow Collins lead, producing an average margin of roughly two points for the Democrat. The state's Democratic lean and the national environment favor Jackson, while Collins's long incumbency, strong name recognition, and large fundraising advantage work in her direction.\n\nExpert raters mostly call the race a Tossup, though several data-driven outlets place it at Lean Democratic, and prediction markets give Jackson roughly a 60-64% chance of winning. Abortion politics following Collins's Supreme Court vote and immigration enforcement after a fatal ICE shooting are the dominant campaign flashpoints. The race sits on a knife's edge heading into the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7341, "margin": 3.16}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6906, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.626, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6915, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5751, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6364, "margin": 2.5}]}